WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.973 --> 00:00:16.882
A few days after the gathering at Ruby's, Anne and Diana were walking slowly near the stream next

2
00:00:16.922 --> 00:00:17.682
to Green Gables.

3
00:00:18.483 --> 00:00:19.564
"What are you thinking, Anne?"

4
00:00:19.924 --> 00:00:20.564
asked Diana.

5
00:00:20.584 --> 00:00:24.867
"I've been thinking about writing a short story," Anne confessed.

6
00:00:24.887 --> 00:00:29.470
"I'm hoping I can get it published, in a women's magazine maybe."

7
00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:31.692
"Oh, have you started it?"

8
00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:34.241
exclaimed Diana, very interested.

9
00:00:34.261 --> 00:00:40.424
"I've only written a few pages, but I've got a suitable plot," Anne replied.

10
00:00:40.444 --> 00:00:43.825
"The main character's name is Averil.

11
00:00:44.625 --> 00:00:47.507
The hero's name is Harold Jonathon Wittington.

12
00:00:48.407 --> 00:00:52.209
I think they are elegant and fitting names for the main characters, don't you?

13
00:00:52.869 --> 00:00:55.450
It took me hours to think of those names, Diana."

14
00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:57.951
"Have you named all the characters?"

15
00:00:58.411 --> 00:00:59.552
asked Diana wistfully.

16
00:01:00.532 --> 00:01:02.514
"If you haven't, could I name someone?

17
00:01:03.415 --> 00:01:05.377
Even if it's just some unimportant person."

18
00:01:05.397 --> 00:01:10.181
"You can name the servant," conceded Anne.

19
00:01:10.201 --> 00:01:15.325
"I think his name will be Bobby," said Diana promptly.

20
00:01:15.346 --> 00:01:18.188
"And you'll let me read your story, won't you, Anne?"

21
00:01:18.208 --> 00:01:23.453
"When it is finished," Anne said, "I'll read it to you and Mr.

22
00:01:23.493 --> 00:01:26.736
Harrison." He was Anne's neighbor in Avonlea.

23
00:01:27.544 --> 00:01:33.227
"And I want you both to criticize it severely, since no one else shall see it until it

24
00:01:33.307 --> 00:01:33.987
is published."

25
00:01:35.708 --> 00:01:37.428
"How are you going to end the story?

26
00:01:38.409 --> 00:01:39.889
Happily or unhappily?"

27
00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:41.050
Diana asked.

28
00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:43.751
"I'm not sure," Anne said.

29
00:01:43.771 --> 00:01:49.554
"I'd like it to end unhappily, because that would be so much more romantic.

30
00:01:50.534 --> 00:01:54.696
But I think magazine editors have a prejudice against sad endings.

31
00:01:55.688 --> 00:02:00.509
A famous author once said that only a genius should write an unhappy ending.

32
00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:03.970
And I am anything but a genius."

33
00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:08.531
"Oh, I like happy endings best.

34
00:02:09.632 --> 00:02:16.394
I think the hero should marry Averil," said Diana, who, especially since her engagement to Fred, thought this

35
00:02:16.434 --> 00:02:18.174
was how every story should end.

36
00:02:18.194 --> 00:02:21.675
"But don't you like to cry when you read stories?"

37
00:02:22.175 --> 00:02:22.715
Anne asked.

38
00:02:22.735 --> 00:02:25.236
"Yes," Diana said.

39
00:02:25.996 --> 00:02:28.337
"But I only like crying in the middle of the story.

40
00:02:28.357 --> 00:02:30.818
I like everything to end happily."

41
00:02:30.838 --> 00:02:38.441
"Hmm, perhaps I should have one tragic scene," said Anne thoughtfully.

42
00:02:38.461 --> 00:02:47.225
"Perhaps Bobby the servant is injured in a tractor accident in the fields and dies—" "No!

43
00:02:47.845 --> 00:02:49.326
You can't kill Bobby!"

44
00:02:49.826 --> 00:02:51.086
said Diana, laughing.

45
00:02:51.106 --> 00:02:54.908
"He belongs to me and I want him to live and flourish!

46
00:02:55.660 --> 00:02:56.480
Kill someone else."

47
00:02:56.500 --> 00:02:59.242
"But it's my story!"

48
00:02:59.662 --> 00:03:00.522
Anne laughed with her.

49
00:03:02.823 --> 00:03:07.245
Over the next fortnight, Anne worked hard on her story, writing day and night.

50
00:03:08.125 --> 00:03:12.287
She managed to include a tragic scene without killing Bobby the servant.

51
00:03:13.168 --> 00:03:17.809
When the story was finally finished, Anne read it to Diana on the porch of Green Gables.

52
00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:23.412
When Anne looked up after reading the last paragraph, Diana seemed disappointed.

53
00:03:25.204 --> 00:03:26.785
"Why did you kill Anthony Lennox?"

54
00:03:27.226 --> 00:03:28.687
Diana asked reproachfully.

55
00:03:28.707 --> 00:03:31.849
"He was the villain," protested Anne.

56
00:03:31.869 --> 00:03:34.150
"He had to be punished!"

57
00:03:34.170 --> 00:03:36.712
"I liked him best of all!"

58
00:03:37.193 --> 00:03:39.674
said Diana, unreasonably, it seemed to Anne.

59
00:03:39.694 --> 00:03:45.098
"Well, he's dead, and he'll have to stay dead," said Anne, annoyed.

60
00:03:45.118 --> 00:03:50.322
"If I had let him live, he would have kept persecuting Averil and Harold.

61
00:03:51.182 --> 00:03:53.104
The villain had to die, Diana!"

62
00:03:55.000 --> 00:04:00.743
"Well, anyway," Diana said, "it's a wonderful story, Anne, and it will make you famous, I'm sure.

63
00:04:01.624 --> 00:04:02.384
What's its title?"

64
00:04:02.404 --> 00:04:06.426
"Oh, I decided that long ago," Anne replied.

65
00:04:06.446 --> 00:04:09.748
"It's called 'Averil's Atonement.'

66
00:04:10.589 --> 00:04:14.411
Both words begin with the letter a, so it sounds sophisticated.

67
00:04:15.291 --> 00:04:20.054
Now, Diana, tell me honestly, do you see any faults in my story?"

68
00:04:20.074 --> 00:04:29.646
"Well," hesitated Diana, "that part where Averil makes the cake doesn't seem quite romantic enough to match the

69
00:04:29.686 --> 00:04:30.527
rest of the story.

70
00:04:31.387 --> 00:04:32.568
Heroines shouldn't cook."

71
00:04:32.588 --> 00:04:36.990
"But that's a funny part, and one of the best parts of the story!"

72
00:04:37.430 --> 00:04:38.230
Anne exclaimed.

73
00:04:39.070 --> 00:04:43.152
Diana prudently refrained from any further criticism, but later Mr.

74
00:04:43.192 --> 00:04:45.353
Harrison criticized the story a lot.

75
00:04:45.373 --> 00:04:50.575
"Why did you write a story about rich people with silly names?"

76
00:04:51.096 --> 00:04:51.636
he asked.

77
00:04:52.564 --> 00:04:55.607
"You should write about the people you know, here in Avonlea."

78
00:04:55.627 --> 00:05:00.070
"But Avonlea is not romantic," Anne protested.

79
00:05:00.090 --> 00:05:02.172
"Well," Mr.

80
00:05:02.212 --> 00:05:05.735
Harrison replied, "I don't like the hero, Harold, either.

81
00:05:06.556 --> 00:05:07.737
He's too good.

82
00:05:08.478 --> 00:05:11.420
He just stands around being polite and romantic.

83
00:05:12.301 --> 00:05:14.643
I prefer the villain, Anthony Lennox."

84
00:05:16.384 --> 00:05:20.708
Anne decided the next time she wrote a story, she wouldn't ask for anyone's feedback!

85
00:05:21.516 --> 00:05:25.738
Anne sent the story to one of the biggest women's magazines and waited to hear from them.

86
00:05:26.579 --> 00:05:27.860
She didn't have to wait long.

87
00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:34.783
The magazine sent the story back a week later with a letter that said "Averil's Atonement" wasn't "suitable."

88
00:05:36.925 --> 00:05:42.508
Diana tried to comfort Anne, saying, "Those big magazines are no good anyway," and that she should keep

89
00:05:42.568 --> 00:05:47.510
trying. Not to be dissuaded, Anne sent the story to another magazine.

90
00:05:48.411 --> 00:05:49.832
It was sent back even faster.

91
00:05:50.832 --> 00:05:53.053
Anne didn't believe the editor had read it at all!

92
00:05:53.933 --> 00:05:59.474
Discouraged, she put her story away underneath her dresser and gave the extra copy to Diana.

93
00:06:01.195 --> 00:06:03.536
Anne never mentioned the two rejections to Mr.

94
00:06:03.576 --> 00:06:07.757
Harrison, but one evening he asked her bluntly if her story had been accepted.

95
00:06:07.777 --> 00:06:12.418
"No, the editors didn't accept it," Anne answered briefly.

96
00:06:13.278 --> 00:06:15.479
Mr. Harrison looked at Anne's flushed face.

97
00:06:15.499 --> 00:06:19.720
"Well, I hope you keep writing, Anne," he said encouragingly.

98
00:06:21.272 --> 00:06:25.375
"No, I shall never try to write a story again," declared Anne.

99
00:06:25.395 --> 00:06:29.117
"I wouldn't give up altogether," said Mr.

100
00:06:29.138 --> 00:06:31.979
Harrison gently, looking at Anne's flushed face.

101
00:06:31.999 --> 00:06:34.781
"I would keep writing once in a while.

102
00:06:35.682 --> 00:06:38.044
Why don't you write about people and places you know?

103
00:06:39.084 --> 00:06:42.887
Make the characters talk in everyday English, and write about everyday life.

104
00:06:43.867 --> 00:06:45.529
But don't stop writing, Anne."

105
00:06:48.070 --> 00:06:51.617
"No. It was foolish of me to try," Anne said firmly.

106
00:06:51.637 --> 00:06:56.582
"I am good at teaching, and when I finish college, I'll return to that.

107
00:06:57.523 --> 00:06:58.204
I can teach.

108
00:06:58.925 --> 00:07:00.246
I can't write stories."

109
00:07:00.266 --> 00:07:04.891
"When you finish college, it'll be time to get married, Anne," said Mr.

110
00:07:04.931 --> 00:07:07.294
Harrison. Anne scowled.

111
00:07:08.115 --> 00:07:08.915
Sometimes Mr.

112
00:07:08.955 --> 00:07:10.397
Harrison was so annoying!

113
00:07:11.358 --> 00:07:12.739
She got up and marched home.
